  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{x}$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing.",
  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}>0.\n$$\n\nUsing that $x>0,$ the above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-1 & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x+1)(x-1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factors ${x}^{2}+1$ and $x+1$ are positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(1,\\infty ).$",
